# Template for conf.d/00-local.fish -- this machine's own fish configuration.
#
# This file does nothing. It is tracked in the repository purely as a starting
# point; the file fish actually reads is `00-local.fish`, without the .example
# suffix, and that one is never committed.
#
#     cp ~/.config/fish/conf.d/00-local.fish.example \
#        ~/.config/fish/conf.d/00-local.fish
#
# Then uncomment what this machine wants and open a new shell. `./install.sh`
# prints this same reminder whenever the real file is missing.
#
# Why a separate .example rather than shipping `00-local.fish` commented out:
# if the live file were tracked, the moment you uncommented a line it would show
# up as a modification to a tracked file, and one `git commit -a` later your
# machine's settings -- or a secret -- would be in the history. Keeping the two
# names apart makes that impossible rather than merely unlikely.
#
# The `00-` prefix is load-bearing. fish sources conf.d/*.fish in sorted order,
# and conf.d/50-tools.fish reads the FISH_* flags set here, so this file has to
# come first. Do not rename it to something that sorts later.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Feature flags
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Each integration is off unless set to `true` here, and each additionally
# checks that the tool is actually installed -- so enabling one on a machine
# that lacks the binary is quietly ignored rather than an error.
#
# The point of the flags is that a machine which does not use a tool does not
# get it, even though everything in the Brewfile is installed everywhere.

# set -gx FISH_STARSHIP true    # prompt
# set -gx FISH_ATUIN true       # shell history, ctrl-r
# set -gx FISH_MISE true        # runtime version manager
# set -gx FISH_DIRENV true      # per-directory environments
# set -gx FISH_ZOXIDE true      # smarter cd
# set -gx FISH_TMUX true        # auto-attach to tmux on login
# set -gx FISH_KUBE true        # merge every ~/.kube/*config* into KUBECONFIG

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything else this machine needs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Machine-specific paths, work-only helpers, credentials-adjacent settings.
# Use `set -gx`, not `set -Ux`: universal variables are written into
# fish_variables and persist independently of this file, so they survive being
# deleted here and will silently shadow it later. Globals are re-read from this
# file on every shell start, which is what you want.

# set -gx SOME_API_TOKEN ...
# fish_add_path /opt/something/bin
# source ~/some-work-tool/init.fish
